.TH MEASURE 1
.SH NAME
measure \- print volume, area, box and holes of parts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B measure
[
.B \-e
.I rel
]
.IR stream.st | \-
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Measure
prints, for each folded part of a stream
.RI ( stream (5)):
.PP
.RS
.nf
part NAME
volume V
area A
bbox XMIN YMIN ZMIN XMAX YMAX ZMAX
faces N plane P cylinder C [cone K]
hole X Y Z d D axis AX AY AZ
flat W H area A holes N bends B
mass M
center X Y Z
.fi
.RE
.PP
Cones are counted only if present.
The mass, in g, and its center are given for a part with a material
(its base, set by
.IR place (1)),
and after the parts the lines
.PP
.RS
.nf
unit UNIT mass M center X Y Z
total mass M center X Y Z
.fi
.RE
.PP
give those of each unit and of all the parts with a material.
A unit is a subassembly, or a stream of several parts, whose parts
.IR place (1)
names UNIT/...; units nest, and the outer unit comes first.
Hole axes are given without sign.
The flat line gives the box of the blank, W not less than H.
.PP
For each solid of a stream:
.PP
.RS
.nf
part NAME
volume V
area A
center X Y Z
bbox XMIN YMIN ZMIN XMAX YMAX ZMAX
faces N plane P cylinder C cone K sphere S torus T [pipe Q]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipes, the rounds along ellipses, are counted only if present.
The center is that of the volume.
An empty solid (the common part of solids that are apart)
has no center and no box.
Lengths are in mm.
.PP
After the records of each part, folded or solid, comes
.PP
.RS
.nf
error volume E area E center E
.fi
.RE
.PP
the bounds of the errors of its volume, its area and its center (along
the axis of the largest; 0 without a center), in mm, mm2 and mm.
The volume and the area of a folded part are in closed form: their
bounds are of rounding.
The mass, its center and the sizes of a solid are integrals over the
faces, by the divergence theorem, and the bounds are proven by the form
of each face and its edges.
On a plane, a cylinder, a cone, a sphere and a torus the integrands are
polynomials, or sums of cos k t and sin k t, along the edges that occur
there: lines, conics of a plane, circles round the axis, ellipses of a
cylinder through its axis, parallels and meridians of a sphere (in the
frame of one of its circles) and of a torus; the rules are exact, and
the bound is of rounding: 1e\-12 of the sum of the sizes of the terms.
Along a ring of a pipe whose tube is narrower than the least radius of
curvature of its ellipse, the integral over the ellipse is by Gauss's
rule, its error bounded through the ellipse of Bernstein of each piece
by the largest size of the integrands there, its pieces halved until
the bound is within
.I rel
of the sizes of the terms.
Without
.BR \-e ,
.I rel
is a hundredth of the tightest tolerance of a length of the part, the
least half range of a parameter of a length that it leans on
.RI ( stream (5)),
over the diagonal of the box of its vertices, at most 0.01; or 1e\-6 for
a part with none.
A face of a pipe or a sphere bounded by three or four lines or conics
takes a mesh where its edges have no such rule: a fillet whose rail
lies in a tilted plane, or a corner of a sphere between three great
circles.
Its points lie on the face and on its edges, and its step makes every
flat triangle lie within delta = 2e\-3 mm of the face, by the most
second derivative of the surface, from its radii.
Its volume, area and moments are those of the triangles.
The space between the mesh and the face has a volume of at most
A delta, A the area of the face (to terms of order delta^2 in its
curvature), so the error of the volume is at most A delta plus the
slivers between the chords of the edges and their arcs, and that of the
moments the same times the largest coordinate.
The area errs by at most the area times the largest 1 \- cos t, t the
angle between the normal of a triangle and that of the face, plus the
slivers and a share of order delta times the curvature.
A face with any other edge has no bound: E is inf.
The part's bounds add those of its faces.
A part made of a shape met before, anywhere, takes its integrals,
moved to where it stands.
.SH SEE ALSO
numdiff(1), place(1), stream(5), part(5)
